313 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 76 0 R/E(A dependency-preserving synthesis can group the minimal-cover FDs by determinant into relations: SENDER\(SenderID, SenderName, RouteGroup\); SERVICE\(ServiceID, ServiceCode, ServiceName, VolumeWeight\); COORDINATOR\(CoordinatorID, CoordinatorName, Hub\); ASSIGNMENT\(SenderID, ServiceID, CoordinatorID\); OBSERVATION\(SenderID, ServiceID, DispatchWave, Completion\); COMPLETION_POLICY\(Completion, ServiceBand\); SERVICE_BAND_POLICY\(ServiceBand, RouteFactor\). The alternate ServiceCode key is enforced with UNIQUE on SERVICE. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

314 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 76 0 R/E<>/Pg 17 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

315 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 76 0 R/E( )/Pg 17 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

316 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 76 0 R/E(SENDER, SERVICE, COORDINATOR, ASSIGNMENT, COMPLETION_POLICY, SERVICE_BAND_POLICY and OBSERVATION are each in BCNF under the stated FDs: every non-trivial determinant is a candidate key of its relation. For example, in ASSIGNMENT the pair \(SenderID, ServiceID\) is the key and determines CoordinatorID; in OBSERVATION the three-part key determines Completion. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

317 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 76 0 R/E( )/Pg 17 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

318 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 76 0 R/E(Each stated FD can be checked in one relation in the proposed BCNF design: sender facts in SENDER, service facts and alternate uniqueness in SERVICE, operator facts in COORDINATOR, assignment scope in ASSIGNMENT, observation completion in OBSERVATION, and policy dependencies in the two policy relations. Therefore, the stated dependencies are enforceable without joining all relations. The derived scope-to-operator-name consequence follows by joining ASSIGNMENT to COORDINATOR. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

319 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 76 0 R/E<>/Pg 17 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

320 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 76 0 R/E(A chase table for the complete decomposition begins with one row per decomposed relation, marking each relation's own attributes with distinguished a-symbols and other attributes with b-symbols. Applying the FDs equates symbols for attributes determined by a common determinant. The witness is the row whose symbols become all distinguished a- values, proving losslessness. In this case, the determinant-based construction preserves the complete observation key )/Pg 17 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

321 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 76 0 R/E(and all dependent facts, so the natural join reconstructs the universal tuple. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

322 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 76 0 R/E(A deliberately incorrect split that places a service identifier and unrelated sender facts into projections without retaining a determinant/key relationship can create spurious combinations. For example, projecting observations separately to \(SenderID, DispatchWave\) and \(ServiceID, Completion\) and joining on no observation key permits unrelated sender/service pairs to combine. A common ServiceID alone does not identify which sender or wave an observation belongs to. )/Pg 17 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

